comments[9]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-22T18:25:57.017Z",codex-root,"Duplicate check: all-status searches for context algorithm catalog, algorithm owner matrix, memory subsystem registry, delivery wave, and idea disposition found the canonical implementation feature pm-4k6b and proposed ADR pm-vvib, but no item owns the one-to-one proposal-to-item governance required here. This task does not reopen shipped mechanisms or bypass the ADR; it makes their state and ownership queryable."
  "2026-07-22T18:26:36.697Z",codex-root,"Portfolio boundary: pm-4k6b remains the canonical adaptive-engine feature and pm-vvib remains the proposed cognitive-memory decision. This task owns registry completeness and disposition only. It must not reopen shipped substrate, accept the ADR implicitly, or convert speculative mechanisms into implementation commitments without independent acceptance surfaces and evaluation evidence."
  "2026-07-22T18:28:19.006Z",codex-root,"Verification evidence 2026-07-22: the new registry task is unique in exact, fuzzy, and parent-scope duplicate scans; it is parented beneath pm-4k6b and decision-gated by pm-vvib. Its linked validation, graph, and duplicate commands were executed directly against the live tracker and passed with only the canonical historical warning baselines. Repository build, generated changelog check, contract snapshot check, static quality, package-boundary, and token-budget gates all passed."
  "2026-07-25T12:33:42.622Z","harness:claude-code","Registry input 2026-07-25 (directed maintainer pass): four mechanisms added to the pm-4k6b catalog as notes (19)-(22), each with a proposed canonical owner and no new implementation item, per this item's rule that an implementation item is created only where no canonical owner exists and the applicable ADR permits it. Proposed dispositions: (19) model-conditioned packing profiles -> owner is the packer slice pm-55ra parameterization, gated on model provenance landing via pm-03pq3o, and falsifiable before implementation by measuring whether per-model utilization variance exceeds within-model variance in the existing usage ledger. (20) merge-probability-weighted provenance ranking -> owner is the signal feature store pm-3hps as one more linear signal, gated on cross-branch visibility existing at all (pm-odohgb federation or merge-preview reads); not actionable in a single-branch workspace, where it must degenerate to identity. (21) hash-keyed hierarchical summary pyramid -> owner is the persistent derived read index pm-801d rather than a new artifact, because it is stored derived state with the same staleness and rebuild semantics as the rest of that index; it is the write-side complement of catalog entry (9) and shares the (6) incremental fold. (22) counterfactual replay evaluation -> owner is the evaluation harness pm-atfm, consuming the pm-lixy judgment corpus and the usage ledger; it is measurement infrastructure for every scoring mechanism and must never be owned by a scoring item, since a scorer that owns its own evaluator cannot be falsified by it. None of the four is independently shippable and none received an item today; the registry should record them as proposed with these owners so a future pass extends rather than re-derives them."
  "2026-07-27T12:57:17.032Z","harness:claude-code","Catalog ownership record 2026-07-27. New pm-4k6b entry, slug ordering-slack-conditioned-urgency, recorded by slug per the ordinal-collision rule this item owns.\n\nOwner mapping for the entry:\n- Signal definition and the degeneracy proof: pm-4k6b.\n- Precondition, since the entry is a no-op until the ordering graph carries sequence: pm-gxs55a.\n- Forecast upgrade, replacing point deadlines with completion distributions: pm-uey8od.\n- Scoring surface the multiplier folds into: the shipped relevance composite (pm-h3no contract, pm-55ra packer), both closed.\n\nThe entry states its own no-op condition, which is the property this ledger should require of every future entry: an algorithm that cannot be shown to degenerate exactly to its predecessor on a workspace that lacks the structure it consumes is not ready to be an entry."
  "2026-07-28T15:06:05.580Z","harness:claude-code","Catalog owner map extended 2026-07-28: new pm-4k6b entry slug recorded-order-arbitration — infer the direction of an ordering edge from terminal history entries rather than trusting the declared spelling, and expose an agreed/contested/unobservable/unconstrained confidence that ordering-dependent scoring consumes. Prerequisite decision pm-4020c5, first consumer the ordering-slack-conditioned-urgency entry, contested-pair governance pm-6x7o, direction evidence pm-jkbqt8 and pm-1w6scp. Recorded by slug, no ordinal assigned, historical numbering untouched."
  "2026-07-29T04:53:19.477Z","harness:claude-code","Owner mapping for the three catalog entries added to pm-4k6b on 2026-07-29, recorded by slug per the no-renumbering rule. omission-cost-asymmetric-ranking: scoring change inside the relevance engine, owner pm-4k6b, and its label derivation reads supersedes, reopen transitions and discovered_from, so it is downstream of pm-4020c5 for a canonicalized kind vocabulary and of pm-wmf72c for lineage semantics actually being present in the data. absence-as-signal-coverage-complement: the partition it subtracts against is the files and docs graph, so it is downstream of pm-f86lth for source-to-item traceability and pm-j8z6 for the file links being accurate; the emitted residual belongs in the omission receipt shape now owned by pm-p258tx rather than in a separate output. pinned-epoch-context-determinism: strictly downstream of pm-10l1vm, which owns the branch-independent ordering identity the epoch counter requires, and it consumes pm-5gf0xf for workspace-level point-in-time projection; it also supersedes the framing of pm-77okxr from a cache-identity bug to be patched into a cache-key design question that an epoch index answers structurally. All three carry a stated degenerate case that must be proved as a no-op, per the standing rule on this item."
  "2026-07-29T16:59:48.772Z","harness:claude-code","Catalog owner map extended 2026-07-29 with two entries added by slug on pm-4k6b: action-legality-conditioned-assembly, whose prerequisite is the declared transition relation owned by pm-mj42og and whose first consumer is the episode substrate pm-li2fj5, and shape-conditioned-parameter-selection, whose instrument is the shape-capable corpus generator now tracked as pm-vv2lti and whose input is the constants inventory pm-sxyj1v. Both carry an explicit degeneracy test: identical output on today's transition relation and on this repository's corpus shape respectively."
  "2026-07-31T14:19:49.090Z","harness:claude-code","Portfolio registry entry 2026-07-31: state-erased-signal-recovery, recorded as catalog note 49 on pm-4k6b.\n\nDisposition: owned by the context relevance engine, gated on a decision it does not make. The algorithm recovers ordering signal from dependency-add and dependency-remove patch operations in the immutable stream, because current item state deletes ordering edges at close - measured as 1 of 1,900 closed items retaining a predecessor against 189 for the untouched reverse spelling. Filed defect pm-xm0id4 owns the deletion; this entry owns reading around it, and the two are independently valuable because the recovery is useful for historical understanding even if the close semantics change, and necessary if they do not.\n\nRelationship to the other 2026-07-31 entry, co-mutation-cohort-inference: both address ordering starvation and they draw on different sources. Cohort inference derives ordering that was never authored from co-mutation timing within an agent instance; signal recovery restores ordering that was authored and deleted. Precision differs accordingly - one is an inference over correlated timing, the other is a recorded claim with an author and a timestamp - so a combined scorer should weight them differently and should never present a cohort inference and a recovered claim as the same kind of evidence. If both land, the registry should record their relative precedence: recovered claims first, cohort inference only where no recovered claim exists.\n\nCross-cutting constraint this entry surfaces for another owner: history compaction prunes checkpointed prefixes, and the recovery fold depends on the patch detail in those prefixes. Either compaction retains dependency-affecting patches or it materializes derived edge intervals into the checkpoint before pruning. That belongs in the compaction design rather than here, and it is the kind of dependency the registry exists to make visible before both sides ship."
notes[8]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-22T18:25:57.017Z",codex-root,"Promotion rule: preserve research concepts as proposals while their decision is open; after acceptance, give each independently testable behavior its own canonical delivery or research item. Reuse shared substrate items for indexes, signal stores, packers, feedback, graph primitives, and evaluation, but record which behaviors each substrate enables."
  "2026-07-25T17:55:28.218Z","harness:claude-code","Ownership for the two algorithms added to the pm-4k6b catalog on 2026-07-25, keeping the registry's one-owner-or-explicit-disposition rule intact.\n\n(23) federated relevance calibration and cross-workspace budget allocation — owner pm-odohgb for federated addressing, sync watermarks, and the remote reference shape; the scoring half (within-source rank normalization, cross-source budget allocation, federation-level shrinkage prior) lands in the pm-h3no scorer contract and is gated on federation existing at all. Disposition: deferred, not unowned — it cannot be implemented before pm-odohgb, and it must not be folded into pm-odohgb's own acceptance criteria, which are about transport and resolution rather than relevance.\n\n(24) fleet-joint context diversification — owner pm-4k6b via the packer slice (pm-55ra shipped the single-agent packer; the joint objective extends it), with pm-3oqjnc as the mandatory co-consumer of the shared frontier. Disposition: active-deferred; the prerequisite is the write-time fold (6) on pm-3hps, because a shared incrementally-maintained frontier is what makes per-agent deltas cheap. Explicitly not owned by pm-3oqjnc — allocation and context selection are separate decisions over one shared frontier, and merging them would hide the interface that keeps them consistent.\n\nBoth carry the catalog's standing degeneracy requirement: each reduces exactly to its single-source, single-agent predecessor, so neither introduces a scale-conditional code path."
  "2026-07-26T06:02:54.811Z","harness:claude-code","Registry integrity finding 2026-07-26: the context-algorithm catalog's identifier space has collided three times, which defeats the one thing this registry exists to do.\n\nThe catalog lives as an append-only note ledger on pm-4k6b and numbers its entries (1) through (27). Extracting the entry markers from every note and counting them shows three numbers used twice by different algorithms:\n\n- (20) is both \"Algorithm (20)\" recorded 2026-07-13 and \"Algorithm (20) merge-probability-weighted provenance ranking\" recorded 2026-07-25.\n- (23) is both \"Catalog entry (23) - consumer-conditioned context shaping\" and \"Algorithm (23) federated relevance calibration and cross-workspace budget allocation\", both 2026-07-25.\n- (24) is both the opening of the \"Catalog entries (24)-(27)\" batch and \"Algorithm (24) fleet-joint context diversification\", both 2026-07-25.\n\nThe cause is structural rather than careless. Two independent numbering streams were running in the same ledger on the same day - a \"Planning pass ... Algorithm (n)\" stream and a \"Catalog entry (n)\" stream - and neither could see the other's high-water mark, because the ledger is append-only prose with no allocator. Any two agents extending the catalog concurrently will collide again, and at the fleet scale this project targets they will collide constantly.\n\nWhy it matters here specifically and not merely as tidiness: this registry's charter is one canonical owner or explicit disposition for every proposed mechanism. Both existing ownership maps in the ledger key by number - the pm-h3no / pm-3hps / pm-55ra / pm-uwfs assignment map reads \"(5) ships in the scorer contract, (1) (2) (6) in the signal feature store, (4) (9) (7) in the packer\". With three ambiguous numbers, three of those assignments no longer resolve to a single mechanism, so the registry cannot answer its own question for those entries.\n\nDisposition for this registry's design, proposed rather than applied, because renumbering a published ledger would break every existing cross-reference:\n\n1. Numbers stay as written. Historical notes are evidence and must not be rewritten; that is the same rule this workspace applies to history streams and to closed items.\n2. The registry keys by a collision-free identifier derived from content, not by ordinal. A short slug - \"act-r-activation\", \"spreading-activation\", \"merge-probability-provenance\", \"federated-calibration\", \"fleet-joint-diversification\" - is stable, is guessable from the mechanism name, and never needs an allocator.\n3. The registry publishes the ordinal-to-slug map, marking the three collided ordinals as ambiguous and resolving each to two slugs. That preserves every existing cross-reference while making it unambiguous which mechanism is meant.\n4. New entries are admitted by slug only. A duplicate slug is a real duplicate proposal and should be merged, which is exactly the check the registry wants and cannot perform on ordinals.\n\nThis is the small-scale rehearsal of a problem the product itself has: two agents extending the same append-only structure concurrently, with no shared allocator, produce a structure whose identifiers no longer identify. The same failure at item scale is what pm-z8qd4k describes for claim identity and what pm-9j2r3b describes for merged state. Recording the connection so the registry's fix is designed with the general case in view rather than as a one-off cleanup."
  "2026-07-26T17:12:36.100Z","harness:claude-code","Owner recorded 2026-07-26 for the catalog entry with slug \"maintenance-invariant-temporal-coordinates\" (added to pm-4k6b the same day, by slug, no renumbering).\n\nOwner: **pm-07pt16** — \"Context recency is computed from updated_at, so every governance write the project mandates erases the freshness signal it ranks on\". That item carries the falsifiable property the entry proposes (a pure-maintenance mutation must not reorder the workspace) as an acceptance criterion, so the entry is dispositioned as owned-and-actionable rather than as a proposal awaiting a home.\n\nBoundary against neighbouring entries, so a later pass does not merge them: the adaptive-half-life recency decay in the scoring section owns the *shape* of the discount, (20) temporal non-stationarity mining owns *era segmentation*, (15) owns *what is forgotten first*, and (22) owns *identity flow across copy, split and merge*. This entry owns only the *source instant* every one of those reads, which is why it is a shared-blind-spot fix rather than a fifth temporal algorithm. If a future pass finds itself proposing \"better recency decay\", it belongs on the existing decay entry; if it proposes \"recency should not come from updated_at\", it is this entry and is already owned."
  "2026-07-26T22:10:56.511Z","harness:claude-code","Owner record for the catalog entry added 2026-07-27, slug on-policy-context-evaluation-in-replayable-episodes.\n\nOwner: the evaluation harness item, consistent with the disposition already recorded for the off-policy counterfactual replay entry, which this one extends rather than replaces. Prerequisites are the episode substrate and the environment story filed the same day, both of which are new territory and neither of which existed when the catalog was last reviewed.\n\nThis entry is recorded by slug. The portfolio registry should treat slugs as the canonical identifier from here: the numeric ordinals in the ledger collide, three of them name two mechanisms each, and every attempt to repair that by renumbering would invalidate the cross-references already written into other items' notes."
  "2026-07-28T22:37:07.596Z","harness:claude-code","Catalog slug ledger extended 2026-07-28. Two entries added to pm-4k6b by slug, never by ordinal, per this item's ordinal-collision rule.\n\n  refuted-and-abandoned-evidence-retrieval - negative evidence as a first-class retrieval class: canceled items, tombstoned ids, resolutions describing rejected approaches, reversing mutation pairs, and prose refusals. Consumes (28) substrate-churn evidence half-life with inverted polarity, since a refusal grounded in an invariant does not decay while one grounded in changed substrate becomes actively harmful. Opposite polarity to (11) open-loop tension; upstream of (14) contradiction gating rather than a restatement of it.\n\n  trust-weighted-context-assembly - per-writer credibility posterior consumed both as a bounded scoring multiplier and as an assembly-composition constraint, with mandatory disclosure in the attestation and retroactive quarantine expressed as a later recorded assertion rather than a rewrite. The relevance-engine half of roadmap horizon I. Bounded below so evidence is discounted and never censored. Distinct from (20) merge-probability-weighted provenance, which measures durability rather than credibility.\n\nBoth entries carry the same acceptance condition every fleet entry in this catalog carries: provable no-op degeneracy on a single-writer, single-agent workspace, identical output and no measurable cost."
  "2026-07-30T17:17:06.567Z","harness:claude-code","Owner map addition 2026-07-30 (evening), by slug, extending the registry.\n\nbudget-feasible-projection-descent — selection over the product of projection tier and row count under a resolved token budget, with an observed cost model, a feasibility floor that forbids serving nothing, and a required byte-identical no-op on a corpus small enough that the richest tier already fits. Catalogued on the context relevance engine.\n\nOwners, split because the two halves have different acceptance and different code. The feasibility and graded-degradation half, including the requirement that a declared ceiling be reachable by the command that declares it, belongs to the budget-infeasibility issue filed this pass under the output-budget contract. The cost-model half, including per-chain amortisation of envelope metadata and deriving page size from measured bytes per row rather than from a constant, belongs to the per-page amortisation issue filed alongside it. The scoring inputs it consumes are unchanged and stay with the relevance engine, and the diversity term it reuses stays with the token-budget packer rather than being reimplemented.\n\nDisposition for the registry's purposes: this entry is an assembly-time selection rule, not a scoring signal. It changes which projection is rendered, never which items rank. Any future entry that changes ranking must not claim this slug, and any entry that proposes dropping fields from a row already chosen is a compaction strategy and needs its own slug."
  "2026-07-31T06:12:42.141Z","harness:claude-code","Owner split, 2026-07-31: co-mutation-cohort-inference\n\nMechanism recorded as a catalog entry by slug on the relevance engine. Canonical owner for the scoring term and its degeneracies: the relevance engine. Explicitly not owned by, and must not be re-proposed under, the following adjacent items.\n\nThe trajectory projection feature owns the ordered observation-action-outcome artifact derived from the same history stream for training and offline evaluation. Overlap is the single pass over history, not the output; the registry position is that one pass produces both, and whichever lands first owns the pass.\n\nThe ordering-evidence tranche owns recovery of asserted prerequisite edges from item text, which writes edges. The catalog entry infers association and writes nothing. They are complements: the tranche raises the authored-ordering fraction, and as it does so the inferred term's contribution should measurably fall, which is a useful joint acceptance signal for both.\n\nThe usage feedback signal owns learning from served-and-acted-on outcomes. It covers only the period since serving was instrumented. The catalog entry covers the entire historical corpus. Neither subsumes the other and both should be present.\n\nThe workspace memory tiers item owns bounded-token summarisation of aged epochs. Cohort folding beyond the retention horizon is the same operation applied to this structure; if memory tiers lands first, cohort folding should reuse its epoch boundaries rather than defining its own.\n\nRationale for keeping the term in the relevance engine rather than promoting it to its own item: it introduces no new storage, no new command surface and no new write path, and it is scored alongside terms the engine already computes. Promotion would be warranted only if the posting-list structure becomes a durable index artifact, at which point it belongs with the persistent derived read index instead."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-22T18:25:57.017Z",codex-root,A rich idea catalog is not yet a manageable roadmap. The missing layer is a one-to-one disposition registry that separates independently valuable behavior from shared implementation substrate and preserves explicit decision gates.
tests[3]{command,scope,timeout_seconds}:
  pm dedupe-audit --mode title_fuzzy --threshold 0.92 --json,project,240
  pm graph audit --json,project,240
  pm validate --check-resolution --check-history-drift --json,project,240
